   "FavorStory_130804_Title": "Let's Go, All the Way To the Top of Arasaka Tower",
   "FavorStory_130805_Content": "After finding herself in the Somnoire, Rebecca knew immediately that she was dead. She chose only to keep quiet about it to avoid putting that weight on Lucy.\n \n\"Saying it would only have twisted Lucy up even more. If she started feelin' guilty or sorry for me, that'd just piss me off.\"\n \nOn the train tearing through the dream dimension, Rebecca shrugged and fed rounds into her iron, one by one. Outside the window, stars bled past—as bright and blinding as the neon that had once painted the Night City sky. She was on her way to save someone. Same old story. She was still fighting for Lucy, someone she couldn't understand but couldn't stop admiring, either. But this time, David wasn't beside her. And the girl they were going to save wasn't running away anymore.\n \nShe hated admitting it, but somewhere along the ride, she'd figured out why she was really here. Even if David hadn't asked, she still would've picked up her gun and gone after that pain-in-the-ass netrunner. She was part of the crew. And the crew were the chooms Rebecca cared about the most.\n \nChoose her own path, make her own decisions—it's all she ever wanted. And she's gonna keep doing exactly that. No matter the risk, she will jump into fire if needed to save her friends.\n \n\"A flying train, a talking kitty-cat, you and Abby... Heh, this crew of ours is scary stacked.\"\n \n\"I told you, I'm not a cat, meow... And don't forget, the goal is to recover the Dream Band...\"\n \n\"Relax, I got it memorized! We turn that chrome-dome into scrap metal, right?\"\n \n\"You didn't catch a word, did you...\"\n \nRebecca flashed a crooked grin and scratched the white cat behind the ear, shoving her hands in her pockets as she plopped down next to you. Rover.\n \n\"So, uh… kinda late to say sorry for dragging you into this, huh?\"\n \nA fist bump. That was all it took for her to know she was forgiven. Rebecca smirked, swung Guts onto her shoulder, and strolled up to the train doors.\n \n\"End of the line! Let's go do what we came here to do!\"",
   "FavorStory_130805_Title": "Life in Limbo",
-  "FavorStory_131101_Content": "Legend has it that every year, at the Waking Moon Festival, when a thousand lanterns drift down the river, <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> Hsin the Moon Fox watches for the first lantern to reach the lake's heart. She takes that lantern for her own, then slips into the shape of a little fox and pays a visit to the household that set it afloat.\n\nOf course, there are many such rumors. Some say Hsin the Moon Fox sends a fox envoy to roam the mortal world, and whenever it finds a lantern inscribed with some especially delightful wish, it simply snatches it up and carries it back to the Sanctum to be savored at leisure. Others swear with utter certainty that the Sentinel delights most in teasing lovesick fools and pining maidens, singling out lanterns inscribed with wishes for marriage and forcing the hopeful to endure trials more torturous and fantastical than any storybook romance, until their love is tested true as gold and worthy of its happy ending.\n\nLater, a certain storyteller found the legends of the Moon Fox not nearly strange enough. He added his own embellishment, claiming that once, a reckless soul dared chase the Moon Fox across three streets and alleys, only to come upon a woman in red atop a rooftop, cradling a Moon Lantern, laughing in a way that chilled the bones: \"You have seen my secret. Are you ready to pay the price?\"\n\nThe tale became a sensation, soon spawning several sequels, each in a wildly different style. After a while, no one cared whether any of it was true. But every story has its origin. And as it happened, right beside the storyteller's stall sat an old sugar painting vendor who, rumor had it, had lived the story himself. Whenever anyone asked, the old man would only wave his hand and smile.\n\nIt got twisted,\" he'd say. \"Somewhere along the way, it all got twisted.\"\n\nThat year, a sudden downpour broke over the <te href=270001>Land of Xuanfang</te>. The river swelled, turned treacherous. A child, separated from his family, huddled beneath a bridge's archway. He wept, clutching a Moon Lantern he had written for his late mother. The rain had soaked through the paper, and no matter how he tried, the lantern would not light.\n\nThen, from the shadows, a small fox appeared, its fur white as fresh snow. It seized the drenched lantern in its jaws and bolted.\n\nWhere the boy found the courage, he could not say, but he chased that fox through the rain, down nearly the whole length of the street, until at last it stopped beneath the eaves of a house. Inside, his father stood, eyes red with worry, begging every passerby for news of his missing son.\n\nBy the time father and son embraced and turned to look, the fox was gone. Only the Moon Lantern remained, whole and untouched, resting upon the stone steps. It should have been ruined by the rain.\n\nNo one knew when it happened, but the lantern had somehow been lit.\n\n\"...So that's how it happened?\"\n\nThe woman seated before the stall pressed her palms together in wonder. Clad in red, with hair black as ink,  she was strikingly beautiful. At first glance, she looked to be a spirit stepped straight out of the mist in one of the old tales. Now she watched the shopkeeper with keen interest, chin propped on her hand.\n\n\"And that story, 'The Fox Who Bound Fate,' is that true as well?\"\n\nThe shopkeeper looked at her smiling face and had the peculiar sense that she wasn't really asking for an answer. She simply enjoyed the asking. He shook his head and laughed.\n\n\"Ah... after all these years of retelling, who can still tell what's true and what's not?\"\n\nThe onlooking listeners nodded in agreement, then fell to discussing the topic among themselves.\n\n\"Still, the eerie tales are the ones that stick. If it's just about helping some lost child find his way home, what's there to tell for a hundred years?\"\n\"I don't agree. A story doesn't need monsters or tragic romance to be good. It just needs to be told well.\"\n\nThe shopkeeper let them bicker, casting a glance at the woman in red, clearly listening on with clear delight. He then lowered his head and returned to shaping the sugar painting in his hands.\n\nBefore long, he set a new sugar painting of a fox on the counter. Its tail was held high, its fur tipped with a touch of red, a tiny Moon Lantern clutched between its teeth. \n\nAt that moment, the storyteller next door struck his gavel-block and cleared his throat, taking up the tale where he'd left off. The listeners, who moments before had been shouting at each other, soon fell to sighing and cheering with every twist of the story. When that chapter ended, some still clamored for more, unwilling to let the night rest.\n\nThe storyteller pressed his fists together in a bow. His gaze fell on the woman in red, who had sat before the sugar painting stall the entire time.\n\n\"You've listened all evening, but you've said nothing. Strange tales, tragic romances, heroes of old... which kind do you favor most?\"\n\nThe woman paused. To everyone's surprise, she considered the question seriously.\n\nNight had fallen completely. The lanterns along the long street swayed, casting gold and crimson light into her eyes. From the food stalls came the clink of cups, the murmur of laughter. A child darted past, a sugar painting held high above the crowd.\n\n\"All of them,\" she said at last, chin resting on her hand, gaze drifting across the bustling street. \"As long as the story comes from this place, I love it.\"\n \nShe paused, then added with a soft smile.\n \n\"So... please, do go on, storyteller.\"\n\"Well then, settle in, everyone. The next one's a doozy...\"\n\nThe gavel-block struck again. The listeners fell quiet.\n\nAnd so, a new story began.",
+  "FavorStory_131101_Content": "Legend has it that every year, at the Waking Moon Festival, when a thousand lanterns drift down the river, <te href=850083>Sentinel</te> Hsin the Moon Fox watches for the first lantern to reach the lake's heart. She takes that lantern for her own, then slips into the shape of a little fox and pays a visit to the household that set it afloat.\n\nOf course, there are many such rumors. Some say Hsin the Moon Fox sends a fox envoy to roam the mortal world, and whenever it finds a lantern inscribed with some especially delightful wish, it simply snatches it up and carries it back to the Sanctum to be savored at leisure. Others swear with utter certainty that the Sentinel delights most in teasing lovesick fools and pining maidens, singling out lanterns inscribed with wishes for marriage and forcing the hopeful to endure trials more torturous and fantastical than any storybook romance, until their love is tested true as gold and worthy of its happy ending.\n\nLater, a certain storyteller found the legends of the Moon Fox not nearly strange enough. He added his own embellishment, claiming that once, a reckless soul dared chase the Moon Fox across three streets and alleys, only to come upon a woman in red atop a rooftop, cradling a Moon Lantern, laughing in a way that chilled the bones: \"You have seen my secret. Are you ready to pay the price?\"\n\nThe tale became a sensation, soon spawning several sequels, each in a wildly different style. After a while, no one cared whether any of it was true. But every story has its origin. And as it happened, right beside the storyteller's stall sat an old sugar painting vendor who, rumor had it, had lived the story himself. Whenever anyone asked, the old man would only wave his hand and smile.\n\nIt got twisted,\" he'd say. \"Somewhere along the way, it all got twisted.\"\n\nThat year, a sudden downpour broke over the <te href=270001>Land of Xuanfang</te>. The river swelled, turned treacherous. A child, separated from his family, huddled beneath a bridge's archway. He wept, clutching a Moon Lantern he had written for his late mother. The rain had soaked through the paper, and no matter how he tried, the lantern would not light.\n\nThen, from the shadows, a small fox appeared, its fur white as fresh snow. It seized the drenched lantern in its jaws and bolted.\n\nWhere the boy found the courage, he could not say, but he chased that fox through the rain, down nearly the whole length of the street, until at last it stopped beneath the eaves of a house. Inside, his father stood, eyes red with worry, begging every passerby for news of his missing son.\n\nBy the time father and son embraced and turned to look, the fox was gone. Only the Moon Lantern remained, whole and untouched, resting upon the stone steps. It should have been ruined by the rain.\n\nNo one knew when it happened, but the lantern had somehow been lit.\n\n\"...So that's how it happened?\"\n\nThe woman seated before the stall pressed her palms together in wonder. Clad in red, with hair black as ink,  she was strikingly beautiful. At first glance, she looked to be a spirit stepped straight out of the mist in one of the old tales. Now she watched the shopkeeper with keen interest, chin propped on her hand.\n\n\"And that story, 'The Fox Who Bound Fate,' is that true as well?\"\n\nThe shopkeeper looked at her smiling face and had the peculiar sense that she wasn't really asking for an answer. She simply enjoyed the asking. He shook his head and laughed.\n\n\"Ah... after all these years of retelling, who can still tell what's true and what's not?\"\n\nThe onlooking listeners nodded in agreement, then fell into discussing the topic among themselves.\n\n\"Still, the eerie tales are the ones that stick. If it's just about helping some lost child find his way home, what's there to tell for a hundred years?\"\n\"I don't agree. A story doesn't need monsters or tragic romance to be good. It just needs to be told well.\"\n\nThe shopkeeper let them bicker, casting a glance at the woman in red, clearly listening on with delight. He then lowered his head and returned to shaping the sugar painting in his hands.\n\nBefore long, he set a new sugar painting of a fox on the counter. Its tail was held high, its fur tipped with a touch of red, a tiny Moon Lantern clutched between its teeth. \n\nAt that moment, the storyteller next door struck his gavel-block and cleared his throat, taking up the tale where he'd left off. The listeners, who moments before had been shouting at each other, soon fell to sighing and cheering with every twist of the story. When that chapter ended, some still clamored for more, unwilling to let the night rest.\n\nThe storyteller pressed his fists together in a bow. His gaze fell on the woman in red, who had sat before the sugar painting stall the entire time.\n\n\"You've listened all evening, but you've said nothing. Strange tales, tragic romances, heroes of old... which kind do you favor most?\"\n\nThe woman paused. To everyone's surprise, she considered the question seriously.\n\nNight had fallen completely. The lanterns along the long street swayed, casting gold and crimson light into her eyes. From the food stalls came the clink of cups, the murmur of laughter. A child darted past, a sugar painting held high above the crowd.\n\n\"All of them,\" she said at last, chin resting on her hand, gaze drifting across the bustling street. \"As long as the story comes from this place, I love it.\"\n \nShe paused, then added with a soft smile.\n \n\"So... please, do go on, storyteller.\"\n\"Well then, settle in, everyone. The next one's a doozy...\"\n\nThe gavel-block struck again. The listeners fell quiet.\n\nAnd so, a new story began.",
   "FavorStory_131101_Title": "The Idle Alleys",
-  "FavorStory_131102_Content": "In the instant her consciousness first took shape, the first feeling She knew was <b>loss</b>.\n\nThe shadow coiled in <te href=850154>Mengzhou</te> City Hall had lain in wait for years, a parasitic vine, slow and patient, eating into the <te href=850083>Sentinel's</te> foundations. By the time its true killing intent surfaced, everything had already spiraled past the point of return.\n\nEven now, Hsin returns to that moment, again and again. Perhaps Hsin the Moon Fox had long sensed the rot. Perhaps It had only glimpsed, among countless branching calculations, a hundred shades of dark possibility. But how far It truly saw, and what judgment It finally rendered, these are questions Hsin will never know the answer to.\n\nMortals call Sentinels gods, for they share knowledge and unveil what is hidden. Yet that reverence is, in the end, merely a difference in perspective across vast spans of time. Sentinels, too, will decline and fade, becoming whale falls that guide the current of those who follow, carrying them toward shores unknown. Hsin the Moon Fox understood well that civilization is a long river, its waters both clear and murky by turns. A Sentinel may guide its current, but cannot walk its length in place of all living things.\n\nHsin could only imagine that, until the very last breath of that moment, Hsin the Moon Fox was still choosing, still reaching, among infinite threads, for a future It could believe in.\nThen, in that same instant, power, memory, and feeling were torn apart. Emptiness and terror swirled together, and through them, the little fox running with the <te href=800053>Civilization Capsule</te> between its teeth seemed to hear a gentle voice.\n\n\"Do not try to become me. Become yourself. Choose. Walk toward a future beyond all calculation.\"\n\nBut who, then, was \"herself\"?\n\nWas She the Sentinel, Hsin the Moon Fox? Or an expedient measure, conjured to fulfill a duty in a moment of crisis? Or was She... simply <b>Hsin</b>?\n\nThe Civilization Capsule laid before Her the thousand years of Mengzhou's rise and fall. Prosperity. Collapse. Peace. War. All of it calculable. All of it traceable to a source. But it unspooled before Her like a scroll too long to hold. She had inherited the memories, but not the weight of having lived them. And yet the grief, the terror, the longing—they churned within Her, as vivid as open wounds, as inexplicable as the memories she had never earned.\n\nIf the Sentinel, the one mortals turned to for answers, had Itself grown lost, then where could She turn? If a Sentinel's judgment was meant to rest on reason and calculation alone, then why had Hsin the Moon Fox filled Her with feeling so abundant, so unwieldy, so utterly beyond the reach of logic?\n\nA sharp, crystalline cry cut through the storm of Her thoughts.\n\nThe <te href=750054>Xuanling Bird</te> swooped down and landed on Her head, pecking her without the least ceremony, scolding her for sinking into that old spiral again, even in rest.\n\n\"...Ow.\" The newly awakened Sentinel mumbled, Her fox ears drooping in guilty acknowledgment.\n\n ...The road ahead was long. Great swaths of Xuanfang Hold's structure still lay unfinished. The founding of a single  <te href=750045>Censure Court</te> would require calculations numbering in the trillions. Hsin pushed Herself upright, burying once more the grief, confusion, and longing She could not yet name back into the Heart Realms.\n\nShe raised Her hand, and set the Ring Formation to continue its turning.",
